{
  "dom_id": "step-q3131",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Use the difference of squares method to solve the equation $49{j}^{2}-64=0.$",
  "worked_solution": "Using the difference of squares formula, we factor the left-hand side of our quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}49{j}^{2}-64 & =0 \\\\ (7j)^{2}-{8}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (7j-8)(7j+8) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\n7j-8=0\\,\\text{or}\\,7j+8=0.\n$$\n\nThen, we solve each equation separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7j-8 & =0 & \\, & \\, & 7j+8 & =0 \\\\ 7j & =8 & \\, & \\, & 7j & =-8 \\\\ j & =\\frac{8}{7} & \\, & \\, & j & =-\\frac{8}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $j=-\\frac{8}{7},\\frac{8}{7}.$ We can write this even more concisely as $j=\\pm \\frac{8}{7}.$",
